using metal shears she cuts out the  shape  [Music]  the artist peels off the plastic liner  that has protected the copper revealing  the glossy untarnished surface  [Music]  she now places the copper above a bag  full of small pellets and pounds it  using the nylon mallet this process  gives the copper an aged hammered  texture  she gives some areas on the other side  the same treatment the hammering also  pulls out the profile of the piece using  a marker the artist draws a line about  1/3 of an inch in from the edge  she marks dots onto this newly defined  border spacing them evenly apart  she then punches holes through all the  dots using the lever operated drill  press she cuts out the space between the  holes and this process creates separate  tabs for each hole the artisan now bends  back to tabs following the marked line  the application of a clear powder coat  prevents tarnishing after a few hours  the palm strands are now sufficiently  softened she retrieves the strands from  the hot water soap and sets them aside  the artist returns to the copper  centerpiece and begins to weave starting  with rattan reeds that have also soaked  to make them more pliable she pulls the  reeds through the tabs that enter  crosses them so that they fan out in two  directions  she then braids cotton string against  the center piece this creates a neat and  sturdy border all the way around she  alternates rows of palm and rattan  fibres the different textures add visual  interest to the basketry sculpture it  will take two months to complete this  work  [Music]  when the basketry work is done the  artist takes the piece outside and  airbrushes them with acrylic paint  she sometimes relies on a metal template  to more easily to find the paint border  it's the finishing touch to the basketry  sculpture and now it's up to the public  but understand the story that the artist  has woven  [Music]
 

